{% extends 'guides/_guide_base.html.twig' %}

{% import 'guides/_macros.html.twig' as guide %}

{% block guide_header %}
    {% set solo = sizes[1000].solo %}
    {% set duo = sizes[1000].duo %}

    <h1 class="guide-hero-title">How Long Does a 1000-Piece Puzzle Take With 2 People?</h1>

    {% if solo and duo %}
        <p class="guide-hero-lead">
            <strong>Two people finish a 1000-piece jigsaw puzzle in a median {{ guide.duration_words(duo.medianSeconds) }}
            — {{ guide.speed_up(duo, solo) }} as fast as one person, who needs {{ guide.duration_words(solo.medianSeconds) }}.</strong>
            Measured across {{ duo.solvesCount|number_format }} pair solves recorded on MySpeedPuzzling, next to
            {{ solo.solvesCount|number_format }} solo solves of the same size.
        </p>
    {% else %}
        <p class="guide-hero-lead">
            Two people are faster than one — but not twice as fast. This guide compares measured solo, pair and team
            times for 500- and 1000-piece puzzles as soon as enough of them are recorded.
        </p>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{% block guide_content %}
    {% set s500 = sizes[500] %}
    {% set s1000 = sizes[1000] %}

    <p>
        It is tempting to answer this by halving the solo time. The data says otherwise: two people do get faster, but
        they also get in each other's way. The numbers on this page come from real pair and team solves timed by the
        speed puzzling community — the only large collection of measured group times we know of.
    </p>

    {% if s1000.duo %}
        <h2>The 2-person answer, in numbers</h2>

        <div class="guide-scoreboard">
            <p class="guide-scoreboard-eyebrow"><span class="guide-live-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ 'guides.ui.data_note'|trans }}</p>

            <div class="guide-scoreboard-main">
                <span class="guide-stat-number guide-stat-number-big">{{ s1000.duo.medianSeconds|compactTime }}</span>
                <span class="guide-stat-label">Median pair time, 1000 pieces</span>
            </div>

            <div class="row g-3">
                <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
                    <span class="guide-stat-number">{{ s1000.duo.p25Seconds|compactTime }}–{{ s1000.duo.p75Seconds|compactTime }}</span>
                    <span class="guide-stat-label">Typical range (middle 50%)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
                    <span class="guide-stat-number">{{ s1000.duo.p90Seconds|compactTime }}</span>
                    <span class="guide-stat-label">90% finish within</span>
                </div>
                <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
                    <span class="guide-stat-number">{{ s1000.duo.fastestSeconds|compactTime }}</span>
                    <span class="guide-stat-label">Fastest recorded</span>
                </div>
                <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
                    <span class="guide-stat-number">{{ s1000.duo.solvesCount|number_format }}</span>
                    <span class="guide-stat-label">Recorded pair solves</span>
                </div>
                <div class="col-12 col-md-2">
                    <span class="guide-stat-number">{{ s1000.duo.playersCount|number_format }}</span>
                    <span class="guide-stat-label">Pairs measured</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    <h2>Why two people are not twice as fast</h2>

    {% if s1000.solo and s1000.duo %}
        <p>
            Halving the solo median would give {{ (s1000.solo.medianSeconds // 2)|compactTime }}. Real pairs need
            {{ s1000.duo.medianSeconds|compactTime }}, so together they put in {{ (2 * s1000.duo.medianSeconds)|compactTime }}
            of combined puzzling time —
            {% if 2 * s1000.duo.medianSeconds > s1000.solo.medianSeconds %}more than{% else %}no more than{% endif %}
            the {{ s1000.solo.medianSeconds|compactTime }} one person spends alone. A partner buys speed, not efficiency.
            {% if s500.solo and s500.duo and s1000.duo.speedUpOver(s1000.solo) > s500.duo.speedUpOver(s500.solo) %}
                A partner helps more on 1000 pieces ({{ guide.speed_up(s1000.duo, s1000.solo) }} as fast) than on 500
                ({{ guide.speed_up(s500.duo, s500.solo) }}), where there is less room to work on different parts at once.
            {% endif %}
        </p>
    {% endif %}

    <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
            <strong>One pile, two searchers.</strong> Both people look through the same loose pieces, and a piece your
            partner is holding is a piece you cannot find.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Crowded moments.</strong> The border and the final stretch leave little room for two people at once;
            the middle of the solve is where a second person pays off.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Talking costs time — and saves more.</strong> "I'm on the red roof" is the difference between two
            people solving one puzzle and two people solving the same corner.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="guide-callout">
        <p>
            Fast pairs divide the picture, not the jobs: each takes their own colour areas and sorts for them, and they
            meet in the middle. Two people working the same area mostly get in each other's way.
        </p>
    </div>

    {% if s1000.solo %}
        <h2>1000 pieces: solo vs. pair vs. team</h2>

        {{ include('guides/_group_comparison.html.twig', {pieces: 1000, solo: s1000.solo, duo: s1000.duo, team: s1000.team}) }}
    {% endif %}

    <h2>How long does a 1000-piece puzzle take with 4 people?</h2>

    <p>
        On MySpeedPuzzling a "team" is any group of three or more puzzlers.
        {% if s1000.team and s1000.four and s1000.four.solvesCount * 2 > s1000.team.solvesCount %}
            Four is the most common team size for 1000 pieces —
            {{ (s1000.four.solvesCount / s1000.team.solvesCount * 100)|round }}% of the recorded 1000-piece team solves are
            groups of exactly four, the same line-up as the team category of the World Jigsaw Puzzle Championship.
        {% else %}
            The team category of the World Jigsaw Puzzle Championship is four puzzlers on a 1000-piece puzzle.
        {% endif %}
        {% if s1000.four %}
            Teams of four finish a 1000-piece puzzle in a median {{ guide.duration_words(s1000.four.medianSeconds) }}
            ({{ s1000.four.solvesCount|number_format }} solves){% if s1000.solo %} — {{ guide.speed_up(s1000.four, s1000.solo) }}
            as fast as one person{% endif %}.
            {% if s1000.three %}
                Groups of three need {{ s1000.three.medianSeconds|compactTime }}{% if s1000.four.medianSeconds < s1000.three.medianSeconds %},
                so the fourth person saves about {{ (s1000.three.medianSeconds - s1000.four.medianSeconds)|compactTime }}{% endif %}.
            {% endif %}
        {% else %}
            There are not enough recorded solves by teams of exactly four yet to publish their median.
        {% endif %}
    </p>

    {% if s1000.solo or s500.solo %}
        <div class="guide-table-card mb-2">
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-striped guide-table mb-0">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">People</th>
                            <th scope="col">500 pieces</th>
                            <th scope="col">1000 pieces</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        {% for row in [
                            {label: '1 (solo)', key: 'solo'},
                            {label: '2 (pair)', key: 'duo'},
                            {label: '3', key: 'three'},
                            {label: '4', key: 'four'},
                        ] %}
                            <tr{% if row.key == 'four' %} class="guide-table-highlight"{% endif %}>
                                <th scope="row">{{ row.label }}</th>
                                {% for pieces in [500, 1000] %}
                                    {% set cell = attribute(sizes[pieces], row.key) %}
                                    <td>
                                        {% if cell %}
                                            {{ cell.medianSeconds|compactTime }}
                                            <span class="text-muted fs-sm">({{ cell.solvesCount|number_format }} solves)</span>
                                        {% else %}
                                            –
                                        {% endif %}
                                    </td>
                                {% endfor %}
                            </tr>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted fs-sm">
            Median times. Groups of five or more count as teams in the other tables; they are not shown separately here.
        </p>
    {% endif %}

    <h2>And a 500-piece puzzle with 2 people?</h2>

    <p>
        {% if s500.solo and s500.duo %}
            Two people finish a 500-piece puzzle in a median {{ guide.duration_words(s500.duo.medianSeconds) }}, against
            {{ s500.solo.medianSeconds|compactTime }} solo — {{ guide.speed_up(s500.duo, s500.solo) }} as fast.
            {% if s500.team %}
                Teams of three or more bring it down to {{ s500.team.medianSeconds|compactTime }}.
            {% endif %}
        {% endif %}
        500 pieces is also a competition size for pairs: at the World Jigsaw Puzzle Championship, pairs race 500-piece
        puzzles up to the semifinal and a 1000-piece puzzle in the final.
    </p>

    {% if s500.solo %}
        {{ include('guides/_group_comparison.html.twig', {pieces: 500, solo: s500.solo, duo: s500.duo, team: s500.team}) }}
    {% endif %}

    <h2>How we measured this</h2>

    <p>
        A pair or team time is recorded once per group, for the whole puzzle, with the same stopwatch and the same rules
        as solo times: active puzzling time only, no solves flagged as implausible, none faster than 0.6 seconds per
        piece, and none timed from the unopened box. Pairs are exactly two people; a team is three or more. A group
        median is published only from {{ constant('SpeedPuzzling\\Web\\Services\\PuzzleTimeGuides::MINIMUM_GROUP_SOLVES') }}
        recorded solves. More about how MySpeedPuzzling computes its statistics is on the
        <a href="{{ path('methodology') }}">methodology page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Time your next pair or team solve</h2>

    <p>
        Start the free <a href="{{ path('stopwatch') }}">MySpeedPuzzling stopwatch</a> and add your partners when you save
        the time. Then compare yourselves on the leaderboards for
        <a href="{{ path('ladder_pairs_1000_pieces', {_locale: 'en'}) }}">1000-piece pairs</a>,
        <a href="{{ path('ladder_pairs_500_pieces', {_locale: 'en'}) }}">500-piece pairs</a> and
        <a href="{{ path('ladder_groups_1000_pieces', {_locale: 'en'}) }}">1000-piece teams</a>. Solo numbers for every size
        are in the <a href="{{ path('guide_average_puzzle_time') }}">average puzzle time by piece count</a> table, and
        {% if guide_paths[500] is defined %}the <a href="{{ guide_paths[500] }}">500-piece</a> and {% endif %}the
        <a href="{{ path('guide_puzzle_time_by_pieces') }}">1000-piece</a> guides go deeper into solo times.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
